 "ga.room": "Room",
 "ga.r0.t": "The first question",
 "ga.r0.o": "Paper · Mind journal · Alan Turing",
 "ga.r0.b": "Alan Turing asked: can machines think? He proposed an imitation game: if, in conversation, we can't tell a machine from a person, what does that mean? The question became the starting point of every chatbot.",
 "ga.lesson": "Lesson for Nesya",
 "ga.r0.l": "Conversation is the most human interface there is.",
 "ga.r1.t": "ELIZA",
 "ga.r1.o": "Computer program · MIT · Joseph Weizenbaum",
 "ga.r1.b": "The DOCTOR script made ELIZA imitate a Rogerian therapist, reflecting the user's words back with simple rules. It understood nothing. Yet people opened up to it. Weizenbaum wrote that his own secretary asked him to leave the room so she could talk to ELIZA. This became known as the ELIZA effect.",
 "ga.r1.l": "People quickly trust a machine that listens. That is a responsibility, not a loophole. Nesya is always honest that it is an AI.",
 "ga.r2.t": "PARRY",
 "ga.r2.o": "Computer program · Stanford · Kenneth Colby, psychiatrist",
 "ga.r2.b": "If ELIZA played the therapist, PARRY played the patient: it simulated a person with paranoid schizophrenia. In tests Colby designed, psychiatrists struggled to tell PARRY's transcripts from real patients'. In 1972 PARRY and ELIZA even met and talked over the ARPANET.",
 "ga.r2.l": "Sounding convincing is not the same as understanding. Clinical judgement stays with people.",
 "ga.r3.t": "A warning from the maker",
 "ga.r3.o": "Book · Computer Power and Human Reason",
 "ga.r3.b": "Shocked by how people reacted to ELIZA, Weizenbaum became one of computing's sharpest critics. He wrote: “What I had not realized is that extremely short exposures to a relatively simple computer program could induce powerful delusional thinking in quite normal people.”",
 "ga.r3.l": "Technology for mental health must be built with limits, not just capabilities.",
 "ga.r4.t": "Therapy on a screen",
 "ga.r4.o": "Computerised CBT · MoodGYM, Beating the Blues",
 "ga.r4.b": "Structured cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) began to be packaged as computer programmes and tested scientifically, such as MoodGYM in Australia and Beating the Blues in the UK. In 2006 the UK's NICE recommended computerised CBT for mild to moderate depression.",
 "ga.r4.l": "Evidence-based methods can work through a screen, if they are structured and tested.",
 "ga.r5.t": "Chatbots enter clinical trials",
 "ga.r5.o": "Randomised controlled trial · Woebot · JMIR Mental Health",
 "ga.r5.b": "For the first time, a CBT-based chatbot was tested in a randomised controlled trial: 70 university students, two weeks, and a larger drop in depression symptoms than the comparison group. Wysa, from India, followed with real-world usage data in 2018.",
 "ga.r5.l": "Measure the impact; don't just claim it.",
 "ga.r6.t": "The world indoors",
 "ga.r6.o": "Scientific brief · WHO, 2022",
 "ga.r6.b": "WHO reported that the global prevalence of anxiety and depression rose by about 25% in the first year of the COVID-19 pandemic. Many services moved to phones and screens. In Indonesia, the SEJIWA psychology line on 119 ext. 8 opened the same year.",
 "ga.r6.l": "Support has to be reachable from anywhere, at any time.",
 "ga.r7.t": "A portrait of Indonesia",
 "ga.r7.o": "National survey · I-NAMHS",
 "ga.r7.b": "The first national survey of adolescent mental health in Indonesia (I-NAMHS) found that about one in three adolescents had a mental health problem in the previous 12 months. Only a small share of them had ever used a service.",
 "ga.r7.l": "The need is large, but the path isn't open yet. That's where we want to help.",
 "ga.r8.t": "Natural language for everyone",
 "ga.r8.o": "Large language models · ChatGPT public release, November 2022",
 "ga.r8.b": "Large language models made talking to a machine feel natural for hundreds of millions of people, in many languages, including everyday Bahasa Indonesia. For the first time, a chatbot could understand “capek banget” the way the speaker meant it.",
 "ga.r8.l": "The technology is now ready for our language.",
 "ga.r9.t": "The lesson of Tessa",
 "ga.r9.o": "Chatbot · a US eating disorder organisation (NEDA)",
 "ga.r9.b": "A US eating disorder organisation took its chatbot “Tessa” offline after it gave risky dieting advice to people who came for help.",
 "ga.r9.l": "Without safety limits and clinical oversight, AI can harm. Nesya has topic limits and an escalation path to people.",
 "ga.r10.t": "Generative AI in a clinical trial",
 "ga.r10.o": "Randomised controlled trial · Therabot, Dartmouth · NEJM AI",
 "ga.r10.b": "The first randomised trial of a generative AI chatbot for treatment showed reduced symptoms of depression and anxiety after four weeks. Throughout the trial, clinicians monitored conversations and were ready to step in.",
 "ga.r10.l": "Generative AI can help, as long as people stay involved.",
 "ga.r11.t": "Nesya",
 "ga.r11.o": "AI companion · TenangAI, Jakarta",
 "ga.r11.b": "We brought all of those lessons home to Jakarta, with one question: what if people in Indonesia had a place to talk that is honest about being an AI, understands everyday language, is still there at two in the morning, and knows when to call a person? The answer is Nesya, an AI companion designed with clinical psychologists.",
 "ga.built": "What we built",
 "ga.r11.l": "A companion for every person, with clear limits.",
 "ga.r12.t": "Then it became N2CIAS",
 "ga.r12.o": "A service for institutions · Nesya, care pathway, anonymous dashboard",
 "ga.r12.b": "One person feeling better isn't enough for a university, a hospital or a company. Institutions need to know when and where support is needed, without reading anyone's conversations. So Nesya became part of something bigger: N2CIAS. Nesya for every person, a care pathway that reaches psychologists, and an anonymous dashboard for the institution. Every institution starts with a pilot, and the results flow back into research.",
 "ga.r12.l": "From research to a support system for a whole institution.",
 "ga.f1": "Honest that it is an AI",
 "ga.f2": "Clinical judgement by people",
 "ga.f3": "Clear limits",
 "ga.f4": "Evidence-based methods",
 "ga.f5": "Measured impact",
 "ga.f6": "Available any time",
 "ga.f7": "Built for Indonesia's needs",
 "ga.f8": "Everyday Bahasa Indonesia",
 "ga.f9": "People stay involved",
 "ga.kicker": "Gallery",
 "ga.title": "From ELIZA to N2CIAS.",
 "ga.lead": "A gallery of sixty years of conversations between people and machines, and how their lessons became Nesya, then N2CIAS. Walk slowly; each room lights up as you arrive.",
 "ga.enter": "Enter the gallery",
 "ga.exit": "Exit",
 "ga.final.t": "N2CIAS: sixty years of lessons, for Indonesian institutions.",
 "ga.final.b": "We didn't invent conversations between people and machines. We learned from sixty years of attempts, mistakes included, and built Nesya for each person and N2CIAS for their institution.",
